> > However, for SNP alleles that are X-linked though, if the locus is monomorphic in Females, so females have only the SNP allele (correct?) then Females would be always homozygous (2 SNP alleles) & Males would be always heterozygous (1 SNP allele from X-Chr and 1 Ref allele for Y-chr), right? So the thresholds work in the same way for both X- or Y- linked alleles?
>
> Hmmm. The problem is that the user might want to make a distinction between sex-specific alleles and sex-linked alleles. Sex specific alleles are on the sex chromosomes in the non-recombining region

so  sex-specific alleles belong to sex specific loci and hence they would be present only in 1 of the two sex chromosomes, correct? so if they are specific to Y they are haploid in males and totally absent in females

